.cache/

# Copied app bundle (generated by deploy workflow)
docs/assets/app/

# CI output — generated by filter_public_data.py, not source. Except
# survey-status.json (fix round 2, R-41): a bare, sorted list of event ids
# with the survey switch on, no personal data -- committed deliberately so
# services/signup-relay can read this repository's own copy through the
# GitHub Contents API, the same credential and the same call shape it
# already uses for keys/events/<id>.pub, rather than depending on a
# deployed example-showcase URL that has never actually been built.
public-data/*
!public-data/survey-status.json
# The internal agenda feed (task 8, phase 6): committed for the identical
# reason survey-status.json is -- `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`'s own
# "Commit internal agenda" step needs a tracked path to commit to. Unlike
# survey-status.json, nothing reads this one over an API; it exists so an
# internal subscriber's calendar client can point at this repository's own
# copy directly. It is never copied into `app/public/` or
# `site/src/_data/` by anything this build runs -- see `tools/convener_ops/
# agenda.py`'s own module docstring for why that absence, not a flag, is
# what keeps it off both published sites. `.gitattributes` marks it binary
# so a checkin never renormalises its own CRLF line endings (RFC 5545's own
# requirement) to this repository's default `eol=lf`.
!public-data/agenda-internal.ics

# Notification body -- a run artefact of convener-notify-*, written only when a
# channel is configured, posted by the notify workflow, never edited.
notify-body.md

# The host's short list of attendance to resolve by hand
# (tools/convener_ops/cli.py::match_attendance). Written only when
# attendance.match finds an unmatched or unreachable attendee. Never
# committed regardless -- but M1 (security audit 2026-08-23) is the
# reason this comment no longer says "never printed": that scope covered
# stdout, not the 14-day build artefact this file was already being
# uploaded as, which is exactly where the leak was. The file itself no
# longer carries a name or an address either way (see
# UNMATCHED_ATTENDANCE's own comment in cli.py); kept ignored as
# defence in depth, not because its content still needs it.
unmatched-attendance.md

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
tools/.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local credentials. Never committed: the real values live in GitHub Secrets,
# and a copy on disk is only ever for trying something out.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Event private keys (tools/convener_ops/eventkeys.py never writes one to disk --
# this guards a human trying one out by hand). Only the published .pub half
# belongs in keys/events/.
*.pem
keys/events/*
!keys/events/*.pub

# Certificate signing private key (tools/convener_ops/signing.py never writes one
# to disk either). Unlike keys/events/, every .pub ever committed here stays
# forever -- rotation retires a key from service, never from this directory
# -- but the rule protecting the private half from an accidental commit is
# the same one. keys/signing/README.md documents the layout and is exempted
# below so the directory's contract survives even while it holds no keys.
keys/signing/*
!keys/signing/*.pub
!keys/signing/README.md

# Raw attendance export (tools/convener_ops/platform.py::ManualPlatform). Contains
# personal data -- display names and addresses off the meeting platform --
# and is dropped here only for one read, never committed. The room link and
# join instructions come from data/speakers.yml and data/config.yml instead
# (R-5, R-6), never from a file under this directory. This filename-specific
# pattern deliberately does not reach data/events/<id>/registrations.enc or
# data/events/<id>/survey-responses.enc (tools/convener_ops/registration.py and
# survey.py, task 16) -- both *are* committed, and both hold nothing but
# per-record ciphertext, the opposite of what this rule exists to keep out.
# Nor does it reach data/events/<id>/attendance-import.csv.enc (task 17,
# tools/convener_ops/platform.py::encrypt_attendance_export) -- the same trick
# played a third time: encrypted under the event's own public key, which
# needs no secret to produce, and committed, so a CI job holding the
# matching private key can read it even though the plaintext this rule
# excludes can never reach a checkout at all.
data/events/*/attendance-import.csv

# Anonymous's own source material for the visual identity -- the .pptx the brand
# values were extracted from, and her rendered examples. Reference only, never
# published and never committed: several of these carry a real person's
# photograph, which is exactly the defect deferred-work entry 9 records for
# docs/assets/flyer-example.png, already in this history. Kept out of git so a
# stray `git add` cannot repeat it. Read them from the Drive working copy.
docs/assets/example_and_template_initial_assets/
